{"kind":"driver:amendment","task":"1","amends":"clause","what":"The Context's re-root sentence — \"the frontier's current lines are re-stated as an edit over the anchor's file, one step before merge_states\" — is implemented in the base snapshot instead of inside the fold. `rw.chained_snapshots` publishes the wave's base over the anchors (per path: oldest anchor is initial_state, each later anchor and then the base are update_state of the previous), so the frontier and an anchored task already meet at the anchor's file; `_fold_text` calls `merge_states` exactly as it did at BASE.","why":"Implemented literally, the re-root breaks a wave that mixes anchored and base-anchored tasks. Re-rooting the frontier rewrites it to descend from the anchor, while a base-anchored task's weave still descends from the base snapshot; the two then share no ancestry and `merge_states` reads disjoint edits as a conflict. A three-task wave (two anchored at BASE editing lines 1 and 10, one anchored at `--base` editing line 3, all disjoint) lost all three edits and narrated a spurious conflict, and materialize fell back. It is also order-dependent, which K1 would report as exit 3 — except the conflict path returns before self-checks run. Chaining the snapshot gives every weave in the wave one root, so the anchored merge is the three-way over the anchor by construction and the mixed wave folds clean in every task order. Every machine clause M1–M7 reads the same; M2's byte-identity against the BASE kernel is unaffected (with no anchors the chain is exactly `snapshot_scoped`).","id":"01M2K9641A00004DTNGCFVF66V","ts":1789501050922}
