{"kind":"driver:amendment","task":"2","amends":"clause","what":"M2's `resolved` test — \"a narrated conflict of the epoch named one of its paths\". I read the epoch's narrated paths as the `path` values in the wave's own `conflicts.json` (`<runDir>/frontier/wave-<n>/conflicts.json`), read once after `foldWave` returns, rather than also threading the `open` entries out of `resolveConflicts`. `resolveConflicts` returns `{ok, reason, transcripts, selfChecks}` and its signature is shared with `fleet/publish-fold.mjs`, which the Check freezes.","why":"The kernel writes every narrated entry into that index — the fold's own `open` entries at `_write_index` after `_fold_until_stop`, and each continued fold's too — so the index is a superset of what the two `open` streams carry and needs no change to a shared signature. Confirmed on a live run: the BLOCKED epoch's index carries `a.txt` even though the fold stopped there and no resolution was ever applied, which is what M2's blocked-epoch reading asks for.","id":"01M2KA93KB00001W49AXXH46S7","ts":1789502197355}
{"kind":"driver:amendment","task":"2","amends":"clause","what":"`driver:wave-blocked` now carries `detail` as `merge.detail || merge.status` (the local `detail` the `waveMerges` row already used) instead of `merge.detail` verbatim, and the per-task `kataMark` quotes the same value.","why":"The event and the row must never say different things, and a `CONFLICT` epoch is now a second producer of this event. For every `TEST_FAILED` return the two are the identical string (`foldWave` has one such return and it always sets `detail`), so no existing reader sees a change.","id":"01M2KA93KB0001VT9PFY6SCCJ1","ts":1789502197355}
